---
/* `/terms/` — the terms of service.
 *
 * Dynamic filename for a fixed address, for the same reason as the order page:
 * `getStaticPaths()` returning an empty array is the only way to make a page
 * not exist. The condition is in `src/data/legal.ts` — these terms name an
 * address for service, and there isn't one yet.
 *
 * **Written to be read, not to be survived.** The house style applies here as
 * much as anywhere: a business that sells clear, checkable information about
 * itself cannot publish two thousand words of boilerplate nobody can parse. The
 * legal work is in being specific and in not over-promising, which is also what
 * makes it readable.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { business, plan, money, addressLines } from '../../data/business';
import { termsLive } from '../../data/legal';
import { assistants } from '../../data/business';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  if (!termsLive) return [];
  return [{ params: { slug: undefined } }];
}

const title = 'Terms of service — Wardith';
const description =
  'The terms you buy Wardith’s audit, Foundation and monthly plans on: what is promised, what is not, how to cancel, and when we refund.';

const audit = plan('audit');
const foundation = plan('foundation');
const address = addressLines(business.addressForService);

/* Stated once, here, and dated. Terms that change silently are terms nobody can
 * rely on, and a customer who wants to check what they agreed to needs a date
 * to compare against. */
const lastUpdated = '9 August 2026';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="hero hero--rules">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Terms of service</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        What you're buying, what we promise, what we don't, and how to stop. In plain English,
        because terms you can't read aren't terms you've agreed to.
      </p>
      <p class="cta-note" data-reveal>Last updated {lastUpdated}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <div class="doc" data-reveal>
        <h2>Who you're dealing with</h2>
        <p>
          {business.name} is a trading name of {business.founder}, a sole trader. Not a limited
          company, so there's no company number to give you. {business.name} isn't VAT registered,
          so the prices on this site are the prices you pay and there's no VAT to add.
        </p>
        <ul class="rows">
          <li>
            <strong>Email</strong>
            <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Address</strong>
            <span>
              {address.map((line, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && <br />}
                  {line}
                </>
              ))}
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          That address is for serving documents and for the record. There's nobody sitting behind
          it — everything real happens by email, and you'll get a reply from a person within two
          working days.
        </p>

        <h2>What these terms cover</h2>
        <p>
          They apply when you buy anything from us: the {money(audit.price)} audit, the {
            money(foundation.price)
          } Foundation, or any of the monthly plans. Buying means you accept them. If we've signed
          something separate with you, that document wins wherever the two disagree.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can change these terms, and the date at the top is how you tell. A change never
          applies backwards to work you've already paid for. If you're on a monthly plan and we
          change something that matters to you, cancelling is the remedy, and it takes one email.
        </p>

        <h2>What you're buying</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> is the definitive description of each service
          and what's in it, and the <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works page</a> describes the
          method. In short:
        </p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>
            <strong>The audit</strong> is a written report on how AI assistants answer questions
            about your business today, what's blocking you, and what we'd recommend — including
            "you don't need us", if that's the answer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The Foundation</strong> is a fixed scope of setup work on your existing
            website. We don't build websites, and this isn't a redesign.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The monthly plans</strong> track how those answers change over time and keep
            your business facts current. Maintain reports gaps; Grow and Lead also close them.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The one promise nobody can honestly make</h2>
        <div class="doc-note">
          <p>
            <strong>
              We cannot guarantee that any AI assistant will recommend your business, and we don't.
            </strong>
            {' '}{assistants.join(', ')} and the rest are run by other companies. Their answers vary
            between runs, change without notice when a model is updated, and are not something
            anybody can buy a place in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          What we promise is the work: the checks are run as described, the report says what we
          actually found, and the recommendations are the ones we'd act on ourselves. Where the
          news is bad or the finding is "this isn't worth spending money on", the report says so.
          A measurement can come back saying you're already fine — that is a successful audit and
          it is not refundable on the grounds that you hoped for worse.
        </p>

        <h2>Timescales</h2>
        <p>
          We reply to anything you send within two working days. Your audit report follows within
          two working days of us confirming the scope and your payment. A Foundation starts within
          two working days of payment clearing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Working days are Monday to Friday, excluding England and Wales bank holidays. Where we
          need something from you — access, an answer, a decision — the clock pauses until it
          arrives. If we're going to miss a date, you'll hear it from us before the date, not
          after.
        </p>

        <h2>Paying</h2>
        <p>
          The audit is paid upfront, by card. The Foundation and the monthly plans are invoiced,
          payable by bank transfer within 14 days of the invoice date, and the work starts when the
          payment clears rather than when the invoice is sent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Card payments are handled by Revolut. We never see or hold your card details. Prices can
          change, but not on work you've already paid for, and not on a monthly plan without a
          month's notice by email.
        </p>

        <h2>Refunds</h2>
        <p>
          Written down in advance rather than decided case by case, because you should be able to
          see the answer before you pay rather than find out afterwards. <strong>
            We refund the audit in full
          </strong> if:
        </p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>the website address you gave us is dead, mistyped, or isn't a website;</li>
          <li>you gave us a social media profile where the audit needs a website;</li>
          <li>your business is outside the area we cover;</li>
          <li>you paid twice, or paid by accident;</li>
          <li>you change your mind and tell us before we start the work; or</li>
          <li>
            we look at your business and genuinely can't help it — which happens, and is our
            problem rather than yours.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Once the report is written and sent, the work has been done and it isn't refundable. If
          you think it's wrong, say so and we'll look again — we'd rather correct a report than
          argue about one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The Foundation</strong> is refundable in full before work starts. Once it has
          started, we'll refund the part we haven't done. <strong>Monthly plans</strong> aren't
          refunded for part-months; see below.
        </p>

        <h2>Cancelling a monthly plan</h2>
        <p>
          Monthly plans roll month to month with no minimum term. Tell us before your next payment
          date and there won't be one. The month you've paid for runs to the end, and we don't
          refund part-months. Moving up or down a level works the same way. One email does it —
          there's nothing to navigate and nobody will ring you to ask why.
        </p>
        <p>
          We can cancel too, with a month's notice, and we'll refund anything you've paid for time
          we won't be covering. If a payment fails and stays unpaid after we've asked, the plan
          stops.
        </p>

        <h2>What we need from you</h2>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>
            <strong>Accurate information.</strong> The audit is built out of what you tell us about
            your business. Wrong inputs produce a confident report about the wrong thing, and
            that's not something a refund fixes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Access, for the Foundation.</strong> The work changes your live website, so we
            need whatever gets us in, and we need you to have a backup you could actually restore.
            We'll take our own copy of anything we change before we change it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The right to ask.</strong> That the website is yours, or that you're authorised
            to have us work on it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Any access you give us is used for the agreed work and nothing else, and we tell you what
          we hold so you can revoke it. When a piece of work ends, ask and we'll confirm what's
          been handed back.
        </p>

        <h2>Who owns what</h2>
        <p>
          Your report and anything we write onto your website is yours, outright, once it's paid
          for. You can share it, publish it, or hand it to another supplier.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our methods, templates and the question sets behind them stay ours. We may describe work
          in general terms — "an accountancy firm in the north west" — but we won't name you or
          publish anything identifiable as a case study, a testimonial or an example without asking
          you first and getting a yes.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the liability sits</h2>
        <p>
          We take responsibility for our own work, and we don't take responsibility for what other
          companies' systems decide to say about you.
        </p>
        <p>
          If we get something wrong, what you can recover is limited to what you paid us for the
          piece of work it relates to, over the twelve months before the problem. We aren't liable
          for lost profits, lost business, or revenue you believe you would have had.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing here limits liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence, for
          fraud, or for anything else the law doesn't allow to be limited — and if you're buying as
          a consumer rather than for a business, your statutory rights are unaffected by any of
          this.
        </p>

        <h2>Your information</h2>
        <p>
          What we collect, why, who sees it and how long we keep it is in the
          <a href="/privacy/">privacy notice</a>. The short version: what you send us is used to do
          the work you asked for, it isn't sold, and it isn't shared with anyone who isn't part of
          delivering it.
        </p>

        <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Email us first. Most things that look like disputes are a misunderstanding about scope,
          and one honest exchange settles them. If it can't be settled that way, these terms are
          governed by the law of England and Wales, and the courts of England and Wales have
          jurisdiction.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
